  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "The longest increasing subsequence (LIS) of a sequence B1,B2,…,BLB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_LB1​,B2​,…,BL​ is the longest sequence of valid indices i1,i2,…,iki_1, i_2, \\ldots, i_ki1​,i2​,…,ik​ such that i1&lt;i2&lt;…&lt;iki_1 \\lt i_2 \\lt \\ldots \\lt i_ki1​&lt;i2​&lt;…&lt;ik​ and Bi1&lt;Bi2&lt;…&lt;BikB_{i_1} \\lt B_{i_2} \\lt \\ldots \\lt B_{i_k}Bi1​​&lt;Bi2​​&lt;…&lt;Bik​​.\n\nYou are given a sequence A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​. You may modify it by performing the following operation zero or more times:\n\n\nLet KKK be the length of the LIS of the current sequence AAA.\nChoose a position in this sequence (possibly the beginning or end of the sequence) and insert a new element KKK into this sequence at this position.\nThis way, the sequence AAA changes and the next operation is performed on this changed sequence.\nFor example, if A=[3,3,4]A = [3, 3, 4]A=[3,3,4], then the LIS of AAA has length 222, so we can insert 222 anywhere into AAA, obtaining one of the sequences [2,3,3,4][2, 3, 3, \n 4][2,3,3,4], [3,2,3,4][3, 2, 3, 4][3,2,3,4], [3,3,2,4][3, 3, 2, 4][3,3,2,4] or [3,3,4,2][3, 3, 4, 2][3,3,4,2].\n\nFind the maximum length of the LIS of AAA that can be obtained after performing the given operation a finite number of times.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\le T \\le 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤1061 \\le N \\le 10^61≤N≤106",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\le A_i \\le 10^91≤Ai​≤109 for each valid iii",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10610^6106"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer ― the maximum possible length of the LIS.",
    "notes": [],
